Wires the new CConnectionTransportP2PWebRTC (see
steamnetworkingsockets_p2p_webrtc_datachannel.h/.cpp, injected as new
source by portfile.cmake, not a patch on existing files) into
CSteamNetworkConnectionP2P, gated on a new STEAMNETWORKINGSOCKETS_ENABLE_WEBRTC_DATACHANNEL
define (separate from Valve's own STEAMNETWORKINGSOCKETS_ENABLE_WEBRTC,
which is their ICE-backend-via-Google's-webrtc.org mechanism -- confirmed
by reading steamnetworkingsockets_p2p_webrtc.h before writing any of this;
that's a different thing, not reused or touched here).

Revision (still v1, no functional change to the transport itself): moved
ConnectP2PWebRTCDataChannel() from a plain #ifdef-gated member function on
the internal CSteamNetworkingSockets class onto ISteamNetworkingSockets'
public virtual interface, always declared regardless of whether this build
enables the feature. Two problems with the original approach surfaced once
actually wiring an application (coffeecutie's networking.cpp) up to call
it: (1) vcpkg's install step only installs include/ (the public steam/*.h
headers, confirmed via src/CMakeLists.txt's own install(DIRECTORY ../include/
...) rule) -- csteamnetworkingsockets.h, where the method used to live,
never reaches a consumer at all; and (2) even fixing that by installing
internal headers too, STEAMNETWORKINGSOCKETS_ENABLE_WEBRTC_DATACHANNEL is a
PRIVATE compile definition on the library target (doesn't propagate to
consumers via target_link_libraries) -- a consumer translation unit
compiled without seeing that define would have computed a different class
layout for a type it never fully sees anyway, but more importantly there
was never a virtual dispatch path a consumer could call through the public
interface AT ALL, since ISteamNetworkingSockets (what SteamNetworkingSockets()
actually returns) never declared this method. Fix: declare it unconditionally
on the public interface (matches ConnectP2PCustomSignaling's shape exactly),
with rtc::PeerConnection/DataChannel forward-declared unconditionally too
(cheap -- only ever used by pointer/shared_ptr here) so the header doesn't
need the feature macro at all. Only the .cpp *body* stays feature-gated,
with a stub returning k_HSteamNetConnection_Invalid (and releasing the
signaling interface, matching the real path's ownership contract) when the
feature wasn't compiled in -- so the vtable layout is identical in every
build regardless of the feature flag, and consumer code can always compile
against and call this method (getting a clean runtime failure, not a link
or ODR problem, if the library happened to be built without the feature).

Four files now (was three):
- include/steam/isteamnetworkingsockets.h: the public virtual method
  declaration + the unconditional rtc:: forward declares, right after
  ConnectP2PCustomSignaling.
- csteamnetworkingsockets.h: same declaration, now `override`, unconditional
  (dropped its own now-redundant #ifdef-gated forward-declare block --
  isteamnetworkingsockets.h provides it transitively).
- steamnetworkingsockets_p2p.h: unchanged in substance (CConnectionTransportP2PWebRTC
  forward-declare, m_pTransportWebRTCDataChannel member, and
  AttachWebRTCDataChannelTransport() declaration all stay feature-gated as
  before -- CSteamNetworkConnectionP2P is fully internal, never part of the
  public ABI, so no consumer-visibility concern applies to it). Only the
  top-of-file rtc:: forward-declare comment changed, to point at the new
  unconditional declaration in isteamnetworkingsockets.h instead of
  duplicating its own copy.
- steamnetworkingsockets_p2p.cpp: AttachWebRTCDataChannelTransport()
  implementation (mirrors BInitSDRTransport() -- construct, push onto
  m_vecAvailableTransports) and the DestroyTransport() cleanup line are
  unchanged, still feature-gated (internal-only). ConnectP2PWebRTCDataChannel()
  itself is now ALWAYS defined (matching the always-declared interface
  method) -- the #ifdef moved from wrapping the whole function to wrapping
  just its body, with the stub described above as the #else branch. Same v1
  simplification as before: no symmetric-connect duplicate-connection
  matching (unlike InternalConnectP2P) -- add later if this transport needs
  it.

No patch needed inside P2PInternalAcceptConnection() itself (the
remote-accept counterpart to BInitConnect()): once a transport is
attached, its own "any transports available" check passes for free, no
GNS-side change required there. (Superseded by the follow-up below on
*reaching* AttachWebRTCDataChannelTransport() from the application at all
-- that part did need a change.)

Follow-up in the same revision: added AcceptP2PWebRTCDataChannel(), the
accept-side counterpart to ConnectP2PWebRTCDataChannel -- found missing
while actually wiring an application server up to it. AttachWebRTCDataChannelTransport()
is a member of the fully-internal CSteamNetworkConnectionP2P, reachable
only from within GNS's own code; the application only ever has an opaque
HSteamNetConnection handle for an accepted connection (from the existing
k_ESteamNetworkingConnectionState_Connecting callback, same as any other
incoming connection), with no way to reach that method at all. Same
always-declared-interface / feature-gated-body pattern as
ConnectP2PWebRTCDataChannel. One more file: csteamnetworkingsockets.cpp
(not steamnetworkingsockets_p2p.cpp, unlike the connect-side function) --
it needs GetConnectionByHandleForAPI(), a helper local to that translation
unit (file-scope `inline`, not declared in any header, used the same way
by every other handle-taking method in that file, e.g. AcceptConnection()
right above it) to turn the handle into a connection object, then
CSteamNetworkConnectionBase::AsSteamNetworkConnectionP2P() (an existing
virtual GNS already provides, overridden in CSteamNetworkConnectionP2P to
return `this`) to safely downcast without RTTI.

Revision (2026-07-27): AttachWebRTCDataChannelTransport() now returns the
transport, and both API entry points bind the DataChannel callbacks only
after releasing the global lock. Binding under it deadlocks the caller
against itself: libdatachannel delivers already-buffered messages
synchronously, on the calling thread, the moment a message handler is
installed, and that handler takes the global lock. Hit reliably on the
accept side as soon as the peer was already sending (whole process stopped
ticking); the connect side had the same latent bug.

